well here's a look at what joe biden's  promised when it comes to  migrants he doesn't want detention  centers along the mexico border  he opposes the highly controversial  policy of locking up children  he also doesn't want parents and  children to be separated  he says he'll also halt efforts to build  donald trump's now infamous  border wall that money will instead be  used for better screaming of  screening of migrants funds will also go  towards helping migrants  arriving at the border biden's also  promised to gather with other regional  leaders within his first 100 days in  office  to come up with a more permanent  solution he's also promised changes  for migrants already in the u.s he's  expressed support  for dhaka a program meant to protect the  children of undocumented  immigrants he's also announced an  immigration reform bill which will  create a pathway to citizenship  for 11 million undocumented people let's  bring in  linda rivas who is the executive  director at las americas  immigrant advocacy center she joins us  now via skype from el paso in texas near  the border with mexico good to have you  with us  linda what do you make of of joe biden's  plans for  immigration do they go far enough  yes that's something that we are all  hopeful for  um that this will go further  um we right now what we're dealing with  under the trump  administration is some of the cruelest  policies that we have  ever seen implemented to essentially  stop asylum  so we know that the rebuilding has to  start immediately  and not only rebuilding but we also hope  to see expansion for protections for  people who need it  so at the very least you're hoping for a  more compassionate approach to the  the issue absolutely  uh compassionate humane uh moving away  from the  idea of immigrants and refugees as a  national security risk  and the united states treating starting  to treat people with true humanity  is something that we are strongly  advocating for  and to hear uh divided administration  begin to plan  for example to turn back the uh  mpp or remain in mexico program  where thousands of asylum seekers were  sent to mexico  into dangerous situations is it it's a  very promising start  what about this issue of of not wanting  uh to continue  to have anything to do with a policy  that separates uh  children from uh their parents  absolutely we need to stop this policy  and additionally start to reunite some  of these families  especially those families uh many of  them who have been  deported without their children and face  uh face indefinite separation these are  families that  you know we in el paso uh have  identified have been working with  um el paso unfortunately was the pilot  program for family separation  so we new families separated since 2017  we hope to bring them into the spotlight  we  hope that the biden administration will  begin to reunite these families to try  and restore  so much that they have lost and what  they have endured what about the  the contentious issue of of  financial help for migrants  yes well you know the the first step is  we need to figure out how to help  migrants in a way  where they are not forced into dangerous  situations  to migrate we know that the laws  of asylum that we already have in our  country  allow for asylum seekers to have a  process to work  we want to reject detention for  asylum seekers because that this is this  is important  for them to begin their to truly be  welcomed  into our country we believe that that is  that is where we need to start  as we said a few moments ago joe biden  is promising to gather with other  regional leaders within his uh first 100  days in office to  to hammer out some sort of long-term  solution and  what should that solution look like as  as far as  neighboring countries are concerned  absolutely well the responsibility has  to lie within the united states we saw  under the trump administration for far  too long that the pushing of asylum  seekers  into mexico um even into uh third  countries as the the third country  agreements allowed  for certain asylum seekers to be sent to  countries that have  essentially no existing asylum seeker  whatsoev  asylum system whatsoever so we  want immediately the the biden  administration to work with these  uh with these other countries but also  not lose sight  that people do need to migrate that they  are coming to the united states to seek  protection  and to stop uh you know putting the  responsibility on other countries but  instead  work together and be the united states  needs to be seen as true leaders in  welcoming  refugees not only in our neighboring  countries but this needs to be a message  to the world really good to talk to you  linda many thanks indeed for being with  us linda rivas there  executive director at las america las  americas the immigrant advocacy center
 

